@flexiarg test-registry/register-the-run
@title The Author Runs Tests Once, Through The Registry
@keywords warrant, author, intent, result, fingerprint, evidence-chain, run-once
@audience code authors in agent pairs, handoff designers
@tone technical
@style pattern
@status draft 2026-09-16 (claude-7; from futon2/holes/labs/wm-contract/NOTE-test-registry.md with Joe's rulings 2026-09-14; implementation futon3c src/futon3c/test_registry.clj `run`, commits 076746d6, 6930dfb2); updated 2026-09-19 (claude-12: end-to-end mint `clojure -M -m futon3c.test-registry.validation register <spec.edn>` lands d41f03eb; durable backend required, 224da745; scope guard corrected to declared scope)
@references [test-registry/warrant-rides-the-handoff test-registry/bind-warrant-to-the-diff test-registry/outlive-the-process hygiene/receipt-then-gate translation/declare-what-is-preserved]
@see-also buffer-cleaner/receipt-then-gate
@how the author runs the declared test command through the registry: capture code and test manifests (path-to-file sha, so uncommitted bytes are identified, HEAD kept separately), the environment fingerprint (resolved classpath bytes, CLI config, JVM, declared locale/timezone values), append an intent, run with a fresh captured log, re-check inputs and environment, append the result with parsed counts. Records are canonical EDN in the evidence store, sha-chained, and the store must be the durable evidence backend (Agency :7070), never a process-local atom — see outlive-the-process. The committed-scope guard checks the DECLARED scope (:code-paths, :test-paths, the run's load closure), not the whole repo: a neighbour's uncommitted file in a shared checkout blocks only when it is inside your load closure, so registration packets demand committed scope, not clean trees. An interrupted run leaves an intent without a result; a failed append yields no warrant; unparseable results are typed-absent, never zeros.
@violation-signature "gates green" in a handoff message with no record; a result appended without its intent; counts invented for a crashed run; a fingerprint that silently skips a directory to fit its budget; a :test-environment imposed on a suite that itself checks warrants — the imposed shell becomes every nested check's observer, and warrants recorded under any other environment refuse (2026-09-19: TZ=UTC alone or LC_ALL alone each produced the same three failures, coherent only as each-differs-from-what-the-warrant-recorded; third environment-decided verdict that day).
! conclusion: The author's test run is registered as a sha-chained warrant with its inputs, environment, log and parsed results; an unregistered run is only a claim.

  + context: Agent pairs split authoring (e.g. Codex or a Zai owner) from review (e.g. Claude); each review today reruns the author's gates.

  + IF:
    An author has changed code or tests and runs the tests before handing off.

  + HOWEVER:
    A run reported in prose is unverifiable (owner-reported builds have been false), and a fingerprint of the full stack classpath can exceed any budget (the futon3c :test classpath reaches an 87 GB sibling checkout).

  + THEN:
    Run through the registry with an explicit, bounded classpath and declared test environment; register intent and result; refuse typedly when inputs or environment cannot be fingerprinted.

  + BECAUSE:
    Verification can only replace re-execution when the execution left a record binding exactly what ran, where, and with what outcome.

  + evidence: futon3c TN-test-registry-2026-09-14 (first commissioning refused :symlink-scope-unsupported on the broad classpath; author entry test-registry-bc9d2129…); 2026-09-16 WM deliveries each carried "gates rerun by me" from the reviewer with no author warrant (e.g. futon2 cb5f8b1288: 86 tests / 9011 assertions rerun); 2026-09-19 the missing entry point was named the defect itself (claude-4 r112: an unregistered verification run "is a claim, not evidence") and closed by the validation `register` subcommand — first two mints test-registry-6c81db28… (claude-12) and test-registry-490a2cc6… (claude-4), both later 404 because they pre-dated the durable-backend fix, which is the outlive-the-process incident.
